  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "9eXWGZSkUsIquYDE9C+w9NV+Qkw=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:52.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Standards history relevant to any construction data product (Wikipedia excerpt in the BIM briefing):\n\n- BIM as a concept dates to the **1970s**; it only became an agreed term in the early 2000s\n- **IFC** (Industry Foundation Classes, by buildingSMART) became international standard **ISO 16739 in 2013**\n- UK BIM process standards (from 2007) formed the basis of **ISO 19650**, launched December **2018**\n- **OpenBIM** (buildingSMART initiative) promotes vendor-neutral exchange on IFC, plus **BCF** for issue tracking and **IDS** (Information Delivery Specification) for defining model requirements\n- **COBie** (2007, US Army Corps of Engineers) captures equipment lists, warranties, spare parts, and maintenance schedules for the operations phase; British Standard BS 1192-4 (2014)\n- Early precedent: **CIS/2** for structural steel (late 1990s)\n\nThe economic backdrop: a 2004 NIST report conservatively estimated **$15.8B lost annually** by the US capital-facilities industry to inadequate interoperability \u2014 fragmentation, paper-based practices, inconsistent standardization. Interoperability pain is not incidental to construction; it is quantified, chronic, and the reason open standards (and products that compose with them) win.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T18:11:52.214072+00:00"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:11:52.000Z"
    }
  },
